#!/usr/bin/env python3
"""pslag — record and measure input lag on PS Remote Play sessions.

The problem with timing a game stream is that you need to know two moments:
when your thumb moved, and when the screen reacted. Because the client runs on
this laptop, the kernel already timestamps the button press to the microsecond,
so we only have to catch the second moment with a camera.

  pslag rig       Fills a terminal with black and flashes it white the instant
                  the kernel sees a controller button. Film that terminal and
                  the game window together at 240fps.
  pslag frame     Pull one frame out of that video so you can read off the two
                  regions to watch.
  pslag measure   Replay the video, find every flash and the reaction that
                  followed it, and report the gap.
  pslag net       Log RTT, jitter, loss and bitrate to the stream peer during a
                  session — the network share of the lag, recorded live.
  pslag runs      Show every run recorded so far, cloud next to local.

Because the flash and the game frame both travel through the same compositor
and the same panel, the gap between them is the streaming loop on its own:
controller -> console -> encode -> network -> decode. Your local display
pipeline is the one thing left out, so add it back with --marker-latency if you
want absolute click-to-photon rather than a comparison.

Dependencies: ffmpeg and python3-numpy. No root, no GUI toolkit.
"""

